Custom Attribute in .NET

A Custom Attribute is a way to add extra information (metadata) to your code, such as a class, method, property, or parameter.

You can create your own attribute using a class that inherits from Attribute.
Real-life example
Suppose you want to mark some API methods as:
- AdminOnly
- LogActivity
- ValidateUser
Instead of writing this information separately, you can attach a custom attribute directly to the method.
2. Create your own Custom Attribute
Let's create a simple example.
Step 1: Create Custom Attribute
using System;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method)]
public class LogAttribute : Attribute
{
    public string Message { get; }

    public LogAttribute(string message)
    {
        Message = message;
    }
}


Explanation:
- Attribute → Base class for creating custom attributes.
- AttributeUsage → Defines where the attribute can be applied.
- AttributeTargets.Method → Attribute can be applied only to methods.
- Message → Stores the message passed to the attribute.
Step 2: Apply Custom Attribute
public class EmployeeService
{
    [Log("Employee created")]
    public void CreateEmployee()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Employee Created");
    }
}


Here, [Log("Employee created")] attaches metadata to the CreateEmployee() method.
3. How to Read Custom Attribute using Reflection
Now suppose we want to read the attribute.
using System;
using System.Reflection;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        MethodInfo method = typeof(EmployeeService)
            .GetMethod("CreateEmployee")!;

        var attribute = method.GetCustomAttribute<LogAttribute>();

        if (attribute != null)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(attribute.Message);
        }
    }
}


Output:
Employee created


We used Reflection to read the attribute at runtime. Custom attributes store metadata; your code or framework must read that metadata to perform an action. 
